    {
      "id": "a02",
      "difficulty": "intermediate",
      "score": 5,
      "estimated_minutes": 7,
      "reason": "The reviewer must decide whether “the enumerated specifics give no boundary to the class” identifies the court’s general-versus-specific coverage question despite not mentioning the omitted Rhode Island statutes. The complaint about “tort claims (not otherwise exluded)” is separately identifiable, but the main assertion requires comparison with the precise coverage issue in lines 25–29."
    },
    {
      "id": "a03",
      "difficulty": "easy",
      "score": 2,
      "estimated_minutes": 2,
      "reason": "The quoted trade-secret provisions make the description’s subject clear: overlap between affirmative coverage and an equitable-relief exclusion. Distinguishing that complaint from d02’s coverage of unenumerated statutory claims takes only a direct comparison."
    },
    {
      "id": "a04",
      "difficulty": "easy",
      "score": 3,
      "estimated_minutes": 3,
      "reason": "One additional look separates the employee-versus-employer access question from d04’s division of mixed legal and equitable remedies, even though both concern the same carve-out. The garbled-list objection is plainly separate, and neither comparison requires facts beyond the provision and passages."
    },
    {
      "id": "a05",
      "difficulty": "hard",
      "score": 7,
      "estimated_minutes": 12,
      "reason": "The description targets the same agency exclusion as d03 but reaches its complaint through a conflict with c007 rather than the effect of a right-to-sue letter. The reviewer must decide whether that conflict framing identifies the court’s defect despite omitting the temporal distinction on which the court’s construction turned."
    }
  ]
